# Encapsulation

## What is Encapsulation

Encapsulation is the process of wrapping data (variables) and code (methods) together
into a single unit (class) and restricting direct access to data.


## Why Encapsulation is Used

* Protects data from unauthorized access
* Improves security
* Helps in data hiding
* Makes code more maintainable



##  How to Achieve Encapsulation

1. Declare variables as private
2. Provide public getter and setter methods



## Example Structure

class ClassName {
    private int variable;

    public int getVariable() {
        return variable;
    }

    public void setVariable(int variable) {
        this.variable = variable;
    }
}



## Real Example

class Student {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }

    public void setAge(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }
}
```

---

## Using Encapsulation

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s = new Student();

        s.setName("Aarzoo");
        s.setAge(20);

        System.out.println(s.getName());
        System.out.println(s.getAge());
    }
}


## Advantages

* Data is hidden from outside class
* Can control how data is modified
* Flexibility to change implementation later
* Improves code readability


## 🔹 Important Points

* Use `private` for variables
* Use `public` getters/setters
* Naming convention:

  * `getVariableName()`
  * `setVariableName()`

---

## Validation using Encapsulation

You can add conditions inside setters:

public void setAge(int age) {
    if (age > 0) {
        this.age = age;
    } else {
        System.out.println("Invalid age");
    }
}



##  Without Encapsulation (Bad Practice)


class Student {
    public String name;
    public int age;
}

 Anyone can directly change values →  unsafe


## With Encapsulation (Good Practice)

class Student {
    private String name;
    private int age;
}

Controlled access →  safe


## Best Practices

* Always keep variables private
* Provide only required getters/setters
* Add validation logic in setters
* Avoid unnecessary exposure of data
